WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.293
You met the invoice agent in the last lesson.

00:00:04.593 --> 00:00:09.068
It opens the supplier portal, clicks next page, and reads the list.

00:00:09.368 --> 00:00:12.820
One night the button stays visible and loads nothing.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:18.846
With no cap, the loop clicks thousands of times, and the only alarm is the bill.

00:00:19.496 --> 00:00:21.688
The smell told that story.

00:00:21.988 --> 00:00:24.061
This refactoring puts on the brake.

00:00:24.361 --> 00:00:27.110
And you'll see that the brake is only half of it.

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:30.325
The other half is what the agent says when it stops.

00:00:31.525 --> 00:00:36.270
Give every run an explicit budget, of steps, time and cost.

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:38.281
Check it before each turn.

00:00:38.581 --> 00:00:42.165
And define what the agent delivers when the budget runs out.

00:00:43.365 --> 00:00:46.863
Before, the only exit is the model stopping.

00:00:47.163 --> 00:00:53.025
The task goes to the model, then a tool, then the model again, then another tool.

00:00:53.325 --> 00:00:55.614
Until the model says it's done.

00:00:56.264 --> 00:00:58.559
After, your code has a second exit.

00:00:58.859 --> 00:01:03.436
The task arrives with a budget set by your policy, not by the model.

00:01:04.086 --> 00:01:09.587
Before each turn, code checks the steps, the elapsed time, and the accumulated cost.

00:01:10.237 --> 00:01:15.627
The model is called as usual, and the final answer is still a legitimate exit.

00:01:15.927 --> 00:01:19.086
Tools run, and their cost counts too, when there is one.

00:01:19.736 --> 00:01:21.605
The task ends within budget.

00:01:22.255 --> 00:01:26.740
Or it ends at the limit, with what was found so far, and the reason for stopping.

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:29.315
In code, it's a small change.

00:01:29.615 --> 00:01:32.774
While true becomes: while the budget allows.

00:01:33.074 --> 00:01:37.413
With twenty-five steps, five minutes, and eighty cents of budget.

00:01:38.063 --> 00:01:43.009
After every call, the budget is charged with the usage the API reported.

00:01:43.659 --> 00:01:47.384
And when it runs out, the agent wraps up, with the reason.

00:01:48.584 --> 00:01:51.553
With the same illustrative numbers as last time.

00:01:51.853 --> 00:01:56.508
A normal run, ten turns, about fifty-two thousand tokens.

00:01:57.158 --> 00:02:02.015
A stuck run with no cap, two hundred turns, about eight point six million.

00:02:02.665 --> 00:02:06.932
And the same stuck run, with a cap of twenty-five, about two hundred thousand.

00:02:07.232 --> 00:02:09.485
The curve never gets to the millions.

00:02:10.135 --> 00:02:12.640
And here's the wrap-up on the broken portal.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:14.501
Not an empty exception.

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:19.317
I read the first page, invoices one to twenty of three hundred forty.

00:02:19.617 --> 00:02:23.930
Next page returned okay, and the list didn't change, three times.

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:26.307
I stopped for repetition.

00:02:26.607 --> 00:02:29.074
Pages two to seventeen are still missing.

00:02:29.724 --> 00:02:32.320
Operations finishes the job by hand.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:34.858
And the trace becomes an eval case.

00:02:36.058 --> 00:02:38.973
An agent loop has two sources of termination.

00:02:39.273 --> 00:02:42.151
The model's decision, and the code's decision.

00:02:42.451 --> 00:02:45.240
Without a budget, only the first exists.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:48.706
And it fails in exactly the bad cases.

00:02:49.006 --> 00:02:54.464
A tool that makes no progress, an impossible task, content that confuses.

00:02:54.764 --> 00:02:59.932
The budget puts a deterministic decision right next to the probabilistic one.

00:03:00.582 --> 00:03:03.306
The wrap-up is the half that's usually missing.

00:03:03.606 --> 00:03:08.841
A cap that only throws an exception trades a high cost for a lost task.

00:03:09.141 --> 00:03:12.341
A cap with a wrap-up delivers something useful.

00:03:12.641 --> 00:03:17.969
I read fourteen of the twenty files, the likely cause is this, I still need to

00:03:17.969 --> 00:03:18.857
confirm that.

00:03:19.507 --> 00:03:22.644
And the budget turns bad behavior into data.

00:03:22.944 --> 00:03:26.306
Every run that hits the cap is a case to understand.

00:03:26.606 --> 00:03:28.199
The cap reveals the problem.

00:03:28.499 --> 00:03:32.039
It doesn't exist so you can hide it by raising the number.

00:03:33.239 --> 00:03:34.352
Here's one for you.

00:03:34.652 --> 00:03:39.215
Among successful runs, ninety-five percent finish within fourteen steps.

00:03:39.515 --> 00:03:42.503
The team wants a cap of sixteen, to cut early.

00:03:42.803 --> 00:03:45.014
What do you expect to see in the eval?

00:03:48.064 --> 00:03:50.177
Cost at the maximum drops.

00:03:50.477 --> 00:03:53.498
And the completion rate of healthy tasks drops too.

00:03:53.798 --> 00:03:58.900
Five percent of the successful runs already go past fourteen steps, and some of

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:00.320
those go past sixteen.

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:04.661
You'd cut today's successes, to over-save on tomorrow's stuck run.

00:04:05.311 --> 00:04:09.800
A common starting point is two to three times that ninety-fifth percentile.

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:13.192
The cap should catch the stuck runs, not the long ones.

00:04:14.392 --> 00:04:15.745
Now the mechanics.

00:04:16.045 --> 00:04:17.876
Step zero, measure.

00:04:18.176 --> 00:04:23.400
Run the agent's eval, and pull the distribution of steps, time and cost per task

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:24.445
from the traces.

00:04:25.095 --> 00:04:26.716
Pick the caps from that data.

00:04:27.016 --> 00:04:32.772
If the ninety-fifth percentile is fourteen steps, and the healthy maximum is

00:04:32.772 --> 00:04:36.028
nineteen, a cap of twenty-five leaves room.

00:04:36.328 --> 00:04:38.323
A cap of two hundred doesn't.

00:04:38.973 --> 00:04:41.631
Check before each call, not after.

00:04:41.931 --> 00:04:45.610
Include the cost of paid tools, like search or a browser.

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:50.221
And use the usage the API returns, not a stale price table.

00:04:50.871 --> 00:04:53.557
And charge subagents to the parent's budget.

00:04:53.857 --> 00:04:55.640
Pass the remaining budget down.

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:57.944
Don't hand each child a fresh one.

00:04:58.594 --> 00:04:59.523
Write the wrap-up.

00:04:59.823 --> 00:05:04.800
One last short call with no tools, that summarizes what was done and what's left.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:07.111
Or a fixed message with the state.

00:05:07.411 --> 00:05:10.639
And the text has to say it stopped at the limit.

00:05:11.289 --> 00:05:13.884
Record the stop reason on the trace.

00:05:14.184 --> 00:05:17.455
Steps, time, cost, repetition, or done.

00:05:18.105 --> 00:05:20.895
Then add a detector for repetition.

00:05:21.195 --> 00:05:26.511
The same tool, with the same arguments, three times in a row, also ends the loop.

00:05:27.161 --> 00:05:28.244
And measure again.

00:05:28.544 --> 00:05:30.610
The completion rate must not drop.

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:33.479
Cost at the worst cases should drop.

00:05:33.779 --> 00:05:37.455
And the tasks that stopped at the cap become eval cases.

00:05:38.655 --> 00:05:39.737
A few risks.

00:05:40.037 --> 00:05:43.770
A cap too low starts failing legitimate long tasks.

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:46.085
You'll see it in the completion rate.

00:05:46.735 --> 00:05:48.521
A wrong cost estimate.

00:05:48.821 --> 00:05:54.065
If the math uses stale prices, or ignores cached tokens, the money cap doesn't

00:05:54.065 --> 00:05:54.871
protect you.

00:05:55.521 --> 00:05:57.152
A wrap-up that lies.

00:05:57.452 --> 00:06:01.666
A partial answer written as if it were complete misleads the user.

00:06:02.316 --> 00:06:04.911
A budget counted only in steps.

00:06:05.211 --> 00:06:10.335
One step that summarizes a two hundred thousand token file costs more than ten short

00:06:10.335 --> 00:06:11.494
steps put together.

00:06:11.794 --> 00:06:13.774
Limit tokens or money too.

00:06:14.424 --> 00:06:16.936
And a fresh budget for every child.

00:06:17.236 --> 00:06:23.015
Each subagent gets twenty new steps, with no depth limit, and the loop comes back,

00:06:23.015 --> 00:06:24.002
now as a tree.

00:06:24.652 --> 00:06:26.384
That last one hides well.

00:06:26.684 --> 00:06:27.572
Look at this loop.

00:06:27.872 --> 00:06:29.722
It has a cap of twenty steps.

00:06:30.022 --> 00:06:30.994
So it looks safe.

00:06:31.644 --> 00:06:36.874
But every delegate call opens a brand new twenty step loop, with no depth limit, and

00:06:36.874 --> 00:06:37.933
no shared budget.

00:06:38.233 --> 00:06:41.928
With three delegations per step, the total explodes.

00:06:42.228 --> 00:06:45.609
On top of that, the subagent's result is thrown away.

00:06:45.909 --> 00:06:49.045
And ending with a timeout exception leaves no state.

00:06:49.695 --> 00:06:54.521
The fix is to pass the same budget down to every child, and take away the delegate

00:06:54.521 --> 00:06:56.287
tool after a couple of levels.

00:06:56.587 --> 00:07:01.825
Then add each child's result to the parent's observations, and end with a wrap-up,

00:07:01.825 --> 00:07:02.911
not an exception.

00:07:03.561 --> 00:07:04.536
One more trap.

00:07:04.836 --> 00:07:10.409
A team writes the wrap-up as one last model call, with tools, asking it to finish

00:07:10.409 --> 00:07:12.197
the task with what it has.

00:07:12.497 --> 00:07:17.665
With tools, that extra turn is the loop again, without the cap you just hit.

00:07:17.965 --> 00:07:21.452
And the text it produces pretends to be a success.

00:07:21.752 --> 00:07:24.937
A wrap-up is short, has no tools, and admits the limit.

00:07:26.137 --> 00:07:28.362
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:07:28.662 --> 00:07:34.371
Steps, time and money, chosen from your own data, checked before every call, shared

00:07:34.371 --> 00:07:35.747
down the whole tree.

00:07:36.047 --> 00:07:41.245
And when the budget runs out, the agent says what it did, what's missing, and why it

00:07:41.245 --> 00:07:41.740
stopped.

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:45.387
That's how a surprise bill becomes an eval case.

00:07:46.037 --> 00:07:51.187
In the next lesson, we'll look at the full pattern this refactoring points to: stop

00:07:51.187 --> 00:07:55.406
conditions, including when an agent should give up, or ask for help.

00:07:55.706 --> 00:07:56.485
See you there.
